<fragment>
  <!--
    Closes the non-product bypass (PR #93 review).

    All FoundryGate enforcement — model allowlist, TPM cap, monthly token quota — lives
    in the tier PRODUCT policy, because `token-quota` is a literal and must be rendered
    per tier. That leaves a hole: a subscription that is NOT scoped to a tier product
    never runs the product policy at all, so it would reach Foundry with no allowlist
    and no meter. APIM's built-in all-access ("master") subscription is exactly such a
    subscription, and any API-scoped or fork-created subscription would be too.

    This fragment is included in BOTH API policies BEFORE their <base />, i.e. before
    the product scope would have run, and refuses anything without product context.
    After it, `context.Product` is guaranteed non-null for the rest of the pipeline.

    The check is deliberately `context.Product == null` rather than a membership test
    against the tier list: every product this template creates IS a tier, and a
    name-list check would need the tier set substituted into a shared fragment for no
    added safety. A fork that adds a non-FoundryGate product must give it a policy.

    NOTE on the master subscription: this policy guard is the enforcement mechanism.
    Deactivating the built-in subscription in Bicep was considered and rejected — see
    modules/ai-gateway.bicep. The guard covers strictly more cases than deactivation
    would, since it also catches subscriptions created later outside a tier product.

    XML NOTE: expressions use &quot; rather than raw double quotes so this file stays
    well-formed XML and `scripts/validate-policies.ps1` can parse it.
  -->
  <choose>
    <when condition="@(context.Product == null)">
      <return-response>
        <set-status code="403" reason="Forbidden" />
        <set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
          <value>application/json</value>
        </set-header>
        <!-- Machine-readable code lives in a header on both paths: the native Anthropic
             error envelope has no `code` field, so the header is the only place it can
             appear consistently across the two front doors. -->
        <set-header name="x-fg-error" exists-action="override">
          <value>plan_required</value>
        </set-header>
        <set-body>@{
            var message = &quot;This key is not scoped to a FoundryGate plan. Use a per-developer FoundryGate key issued against a quota tier product.&quot;;
            if (context.Api.Id == &quot;__ANTHROPIC_API_ID__&quot;) {
                return new JObject(
                    new JProperty(&quot;type&quot;, &quot;error&quot;),
                    new JProperty(&quot;error&quot;, new JObject(
                        new JProperty(&quot;type&quot;, &quot;permission_error&quot;),
                        new JProperty(&quot;message&quot;, message)))
                ).ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
            }
            return new JObject(
                new JProperty(&quot;error&quot;, new JObject(
                    new JProperty(&quot;code&quot;, &quot;plan_required&quot;),
                    new JProperty(&quot;type&quot;, &quot;invalid_request_error&quot;),
                    new JProperty(&quot;message&quot;, message)))
            ).ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
        }</set-body>
      </return-response>
    </when>
  </choose>
</fragment>
